<main id="main-content" class="sc-wrap">
  <div class="sc-hero">
    <div class="sc-eyebrow"><div class="sc-dot"></div><div class="sc-label">TOUCHOSC LAYOUT · FREE</div></div>
    <h1>Play LOOM from your iPad.</h1>
    <p>This TouchOSC layout gives every LOOM track its own pads on an iPad: record, play, stop, undo, redo and clear, laid out like LOOM's looper view, with a tile of global controls beside them.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sc-section" style="padding-top:56px;">
    <img class="sc-shot" src="/assets/loom/touchosc-ipad.webp" alt="The LOOM layout in TouchOSC on an iPad: five audio track tiles on the top row, four instrument track tiles and a GLOBAL tile on the bottom row" width="1600" height="1200" loading="eager">
  </div>

  <div class="sc-pdfs">
    <div class="sc-card" style="background:linear-gradient(180deg, #12201A, #0F1114);">
      <div class="sc-label" style="color:#35D07F;">THE LAYOUT</div>
      <h3>LOOM.tosc</h3>
      <p>Open it in TouchOSC on your iPad. It works with LOOM's default MIDI settings: channel 16 and the 1.x layout.</p>
      <div class="sc-spacer"></div>
      <a class="sc-btn sc-btn-green" href="/assets/loom/LOOM.tosc" download>DOWNLOAD LOOM.TOSC</a>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-card">
      <div class="sc-label" style="color:#4CC9F0;">WHAT ELSE YOU NEED</div>
      <h3>Two apps from hexler</h3>
      <p><strong style="color:#E8EAED;">TouchOSC</strong> on the iPad, a paid app. <strong style="color:#E8EAED;">TouchOSC Bridge</strong> on the Mac running LOOM, which is free and carries MIDI between them over your network. Keep both devices on the same network.</p>
      <div class="sc-spacer"></div>
      <a class="sc-btn sc-btn-ghost" href="https://hexler.net/touchosc#resources">GET TOUCHOSC BRIDGE</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="sc-section">
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px;">
      <div class="sc-label">SETUP</div>
      <h2 class="sc-h">Connect it in six steps.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="sc-steps">
      <li><div>On the Mac, open <strong>TouchOSC Bridge</strong>. LOOM picks it up as a MIDI source on its own, with no setting to change. If LOOM is already open, its notice row says a MIDI device was connected.</div></li>
      <li><div>Send <span class="sc-code">LOOM.tosc</span> to the iPad, with AirDrop for example, and open it in TouchOSC.</div></li>
      <li><div>In TouchOSC, open the connections settings (the chain-link icon). Under <strong>Bridge</strong>, turn on connection 1 and set its host to your Mac.</div></li>
      <li><div>Under <strong>MIDI</strong>, turn on connection 1 and set its send port to <span class="sc-code">&lt;Bridge 1&gt;</span>. This is the step people miss: the Bridge connection sends nothing until a MIDI connection uses it.</div></li>
      <li><div>Leave the editor so TouchOSC is in play mode.</div></li>
      <li><div>Tap track 1's <strong>REC / PLAY / DUB</strong> pad and watch the <span class="sc-code">MIDI</span> readout on LOOM's bottom rail. It should read <span class="sc-code">16 · N0 · 127 ✓</span>, and track 1 starts recording.</div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="sc-section">
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px;">
      <div class="sc-label">THE PADS</div>
      <h2 class="sc-h">What each pad does.</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="sc-list">
      <li><strong>REC / PLAY / DUB</strong> does what the track's ring does: records an empty track, closes the loop, overdubs a playing track, and plays a stopped one.</li>
      <li><strong>PLAY</strong> and <strong>STOP</strong> play and stop that track on its own quantize setting.</li>
      <li><strong>UNDO</strong> and <strong>REDO</strong> take the last layer off the track and put it back.</li>
      <li><strong>CLEAR</strong> clears the track when you hold it for one second. A short tap does nothing, so a stray touch cannot lose a take.</li>
      <li>The <strong>GLOBAL</strong> tile has ALL START, ALL STOP, TRANSPORT START, TRANSPORT STOP, PERFORM FROM TOP, and ALL CLEAR (also a one-second hold).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="sc-callout">The pads follow LOOM's built-in MIDI layout, which the <a href="/loom/manual/">LOOM manual</a> lists in full on its MIDI mapping page. A hardware pad controller set to the same notes does the same things.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sc-section">
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px;">
      <div class="sc-label">IF NOTHING HAPPENS</div>
      <h2 class="sc-h">Read the MIDI readout.</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="sc-list">
      <li><strong>It still shows a dash.</strong> No MIDI is reaching LOOM. Check that MIDI connection 1's send port is <span class="sc-code">&lt;Bridge 1&gt;</span>, that the Bridge connection is on with your Mac as its host, and that TouchOSC Bridge is running on the Mac.</li>
      <li><strong>The line ends in a dot instead of a tick.</strong> LOOM hears the iPad but the note is not one of its commands. In LOOM's side panel, set MIDI CH to 16 and MIDI MAP to 1.x layout.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="sc-callout">LOOM for Windows is on its way. TouchOSC Bridge runs on Windows too, and this page will cover it when LOOM for Windows ships.</p>
  </div>

